            <ArticleTitle>[Identification of a novel ABO B(A)07 allele].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>894-896</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3760/cma.j.issn.1003-9406.2017.06.025</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To explore the molecular basis for a novel B(A) phenotype.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Genomic DNA was abstracted from peripheral blood sample from the proband. ABO genotyping were carried out with sequence specific primer PCR (PCR-SSP). Exons 6 and 7 of the ABO gene were amplified with PCR and sequenced.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Anti-A serum could not be adsorbed or eluted by the donor's red blood cells, and no irregular antibodies were found in the plasma. PCR-SSP showed that the ABO genotype of the donor was ABO *B/O. Sequencing results showed that one of the alleles was ABO *O02, while the other could not be defined but contained the following mutation points, 297A&gt;G, 526C&gt;G, 657C&gt;T, 701C&gt;T, 703G&gt;A, 796C&gt;A, 803G&gt;C, and 930G&gt;A. The data was accepted by the GenBank (the loading code was KM974887) and the Blood Group Antigen Mutation Database, and was confirmed to be a novel allele of B(A).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A novel allele ABO *B(A)07 with 701C&gt;T has been identified, which may facilitate further study on blood antigen variants and typing of the blood groups.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
